Microscopic Colitis (Mild Lymphocytic)
Autoimmune Conditions Mild Severity
Normal colonoscopy appearance, histologically confirmed lymphocytosis, non-debilitating

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AMS-028
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lower abdominal cramping and persistent watery diarrhea causing extreme distress and fear of bowel obstruction or severe infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute, intense abdominal cramping accompanied by continuous loose stools. Immediate clinical review was necessary to differentiate an acute exacerbation of known functional/mild autoimmune bowel disease from acute mechanical bowel obstruction or visceral perforation prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My stomach is in knots, the pain is coming in intense waves, and I'm passing nothing but water. I feel like my bowel is completely blocked and going to rupture.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports intermittent mild watery diarrhea which is usually well-tolerated. Today, following a heavy, rich meal, the patient experienced an acute escalation of symptoms beginning 4 hours ago. Symptoms involve severe, colicky lower abdominal pain preceding explosive, non-bloody watery diarrhea. The intensity of the spasms triggered severe health anxiety regarding a surgical emergency. Denies nausea, vomiting, localized right lower quadrant pain, or fevers.
Functional Status: Independent
Social/Safety Concerns: Low risk.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate discomfort during cramping episodes, restless.
Physical Exam: Abdomen is non-distended and soft. Bowel sounds are hyperactive. There is mild diffuse tenderness in the lower quadrants but absolutely no rigidity, guarding, or rebound tenderness. Percussion is normal. Cardiovascular exam confirms patient is well-perfused with moist mucous membranes. Previous records indicate a normal colonoscopy appearance, histologically confirmed lymphocytosis, non-debilitating historically.
Environmental Observations: Tidy environment, comfortable temperature.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient takes no regular gastrointestinal medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute colicky spasm in the setting of Microscopic Colitis (Mild Lymphocytic) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Small Bowel Obstruction - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Passing abundant liquid stool/flatus, absence of vomiting, and no abdominal distension or high-pitched tinkling bowel sounds.

Acute Appendicitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete lack of localized peritonitis in the right iliac fossa, afebrile status, and normal appetite prior to the event.

Inflammatory Bowel Disease (Severe Crohn's/UC flare) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of gross blood or mucus in the stool, and known recent normal macroscopic colonoscopy.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with intense, colicky abdominal pain mimicking a potential surgical emergency. Triage correctly prioritized a physical examination to definitively rule out mechanical obstruction or early peritonitis. Bedside evaluation confirmed a soft, non-surgical abdomen with hyperactive motility, indicating severe smooth muscle spasm secondary to a known mild autoimmune colitis flare. The rapid clinical exclusion of a surgical abdomen safely alleviated the patient's severe panic and entirely prevented an unnecessary presentation to the emergency department for an abdominal CT scan, satisfying the criteria for urgent assessment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered an oral antispasmodic (hyoscine butylbromide) to relieve acute visceral smooth muscle cramping. Reassured regarding hydration status.
Positive Behavioral Change: Place a heated wheat pack firmly over your lower abdomen and lie on your left side with your knees drawn up. Applying direct heat and reducing abdominal wall tension will immediately interrupt the pain signals from the bowel spasms, allowing the antispasmodic medication to work faster.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP appointment in 1-2 weeks to consider budesonide trial if symptoms remain refractory to lifestyle measures.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if the pain shifts to a constant, localized sharp pain, if your abdomen becomes hard and distended, or if you begin vomiting repetitively.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Lymphocytic colitis causes the bowel to spasm and secrete water due to microscopic immune cells. It is intensely uncomfortable but does not damage the structure of the bowel or cause blockages.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if you pass visible blood in the toilet bowl, or if you develop a high fever and chills.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your bowel is experiencing a 'charley horse' or severe muscle cramp because the lining is irritated. The pain is not from a blockage. The antispasmodic will forcibly relax the bowel muscle.
Actionable Steps: Rest with the heat pack tonight. Stick to a bland diet (toast, rice, bananas) for the next 24 hours to give the bowel lining time to settle.

SMS INFORMATION

Dx: Bowel spasm due to Microscopic Colitis. Abdomen is soft, no signs of blockage or surgical emergency. Antispasmodic given. See GP in 1-2 weeks.

